Okay, let's see. Oliver has to put away books at the library. The total number of books is 46, but a librarian takes 10 of them. So first, I need to figure out how many books Oliver has left to put away. 

So, subtracting the 10 books that the librarian took from the total 46. Let me do that calculation: 46 minus 10 equals 36. So Oliver has 36 books to put away. 

Now, the problem says that Oliver can fit 4 books on a shelf. I need to find out how many shelves he will need. To do this, I should divide the number of books by the number of books per shelf. So that would be 36 divided by 4. Let me calculate that. 

36 divided by 4 is 9. Wait, is that right? Let me check. 4 times 9 is 36, yes. So that means Oliver needs 9 shelves. 

But wait, hold on. Sometimes when you divide, if there's a remainder, you need to round up. For example, if it was 37 books, 37 divided by 4 is 9.25, so you'd need 10 shelves. But in this case, 36 divided by 4 is exactly 9. So there's no remainder. Therefore, Oliver needs exactly 9 shelves. 

